        "longQuestions": [
          {
            "question": "Describe the construction, working principle, least count calculation, and zero error correction of Vernier Callipers with neat sketches.",
            "answer": "1. Construction: Vernier Callipers consists of two steel jaws. One jaw is fixed to the main scale graduated in millimeters; the other jaw slides with the vernier scale. It has lower jaws for external dimensions, upper jaws for internal diameters, and a thin stem for measuring depth.\n2. Least Count: Least Count = Smallest Division on Main Scale / Number of Divisions on Vernier Scale = $1\\text{ mm} / 10 = 0.1\\text{ mm} = 0.01\\text{ cm}$.\n3. Zero Error Correction: Close the jaws gently. If the vernier 0 coincides with the main scale 0, zero error is nil. If vernier 0 is to the right of main scale 0, zero error is positive (e.g., coinciding line 3 means $+0.03\\text{ cm}$, which must be subtracted from final readings). If vernier 0 is to the left, zero error is negative (e.g., coinciding line 7 means $-(10-7) \\times 0.01 = -0.03\\text{ cm}$, which must be added).\n4. Total Measurement Formula: $\\text{Observed Reading} = \\text{Main Scale Reading} + (\\text{Coinciding Vernier Division} \\times \\text{Least Count})$. $\\text{Corrected Reading} = \\text{Observed Reading} - (\\pm \\text{Zero Error})$.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 1 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Discuss in detail the relationship between Quran and Physics, citing relevant Ayaat and their scientific implications.",
            "answer": "1. Theological and Empirical Foundation: The Holy Quran contains over 700 verses urging humanity to investigate natural phenomena and cosmic design, emphasizing that natural laws reflect Divine order and wisdom.\n2. Surah Yaseen (36:40) & Celestial Mechanics: 'It is not allowable for the sun to reach the moon, nor does the night overtake the day, but each, in an orbit, is swimming.' Centuries before Kepler's laws of planetary motion, this verse revealed that celestial bodies move along designated gravitational trajectories without mutual destruction.\n3. Surah Ar-Rahman (55:33) & Space Exploration / Escape Velocity: 'O company of jinn and mankind, if you are able to pass beyond the regions of heavens and earth, then pass. You will not pass except by authority (power).' In modern astronautics, penetrating Earth's gravitational field requires exceeding escape velocity ($11.2\\text{ km/s}$) using powerful rocket propellant energy (سلطان).\n4. Surah Al-Mulk (67:3) & Cosmic Symmetry: 'He who created seven heavens in layers. You see no discrepancy in the creation of the Compassionate. Look again; do you see any rift?' This highlights the universal symmetry and mathematical perfection governing physical laws from subatomic quarks to galaxy clusters.\n5. Epistemological Reflection: The repeated Quranic phrases أَفَلَا يَتَفَكَّرُونَ, أَفَلَا يَتَدَبَّرُونَ, and أَفَلَا يَنظُرُونَ mandate empirical research, observation, and deductive reasoning.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 1 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Explain the Micrometer Screw Gauge in detail: construction, pitch, least count derivation, zero error determination, and experimental steps to measure wire diameter.",
            "answer": "1. Construction: Consists of a U-shaped metallic frame with a fixed anvil on one end and a movable spindle connected to a threaded screw inside a cylindrical sleeve (main scale) and thimble (circular scale). A ratchet mechanism prevents excessive pressure on the object.\n2. Pitch and Least Count: Pitch is the linear distance traversed along the main scale in one complete turn of the thimble ($1\\text{ mm}$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$). Least Count = $\\text{Pitch} / \\text{Total Circular Scale Divisions} = 1\\text{ mm} / 100 = 0.01\\text{ mm} = 0.001\\text{ cm}$.\n3. Zero Error: When anvil and spindle touch: if 0 of circular scale coincides with index line, zero error is zero. If circular 0 is below index line, error is positive (subtract). If circular 0 is above index line, error is negative (add).\n4. Procedure: Place the wire between anvil and spindle. Turn ratchet until it clicks three times. Read the main scale reading (MSR) and the coinciding circular division (CSR). Calculate $\\text{Total Diameter} = \\text{MSR} + (\\text{CSR} \\times \\text{LC}) - (\\pm \\text{Zero Error})$.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 1 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Elaborate the historical contributions of Muslim scientists to physical sciences, focusing on Al-Kindi, Ibn al-Haitham, and Al-Biruni.",
            "answer": "1. Yaqub Ibn Ishaq al-Kindi (800–873 AD): Known as the 'Philosopher of the Arabs'. Authored extensive treatises on geometric optics, refraction of light, tidal phenomena caused by the moon, and music acoustics based on string frequencies.\n2. Ibn al-Haitham (965–1039 AD): Recognized as the true founder of modern experimental optics. In his masterwork 'Kitab al-Manazir', he disproved Ptolemy and Euclid's emission hypothesis (that light emerges from the eye) and proved that light enters the eye after reflecting from objects. He formulated laws of reflection and refraction, investigated spherical aberration, atmospheric twilight refraction, and built the camera obscura (pinhole camera).\n3. Abu Rayhan al-Biruni (973–1048 AD): Pioneer in experimental physics, geodesy, and astronomy. Developed a revolutionary method to calculate Earth's radius from a mountain height and angle of dip of the horizon ($R = \\frac{h \\cos\\theta}{1 - \\cos\\theta}$), determining $R = 6335.7\\text{ km}$ at Nandana Fort. He also determined the specific gravities of 18 metals and gems using a custom hydrostatic vessel with remarkable accuracy.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 1 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "What are Significant Figures? Explain all rules for identifying significant figures and the rules for rounding off in addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.",
            "answer": "1. Definition: In any physical measurement, significant figures are all accurately known digits plus the first doubtful (estimated) digit. They signify the precision of the measuring device.\n2. Rules for Identification:\n   a. All non-zero digits are significant ($4.23$ has 3).\n   b. Zeros between non-zero digits are significant ($2008$ has 4).\n   c. Leading zeros preceding the first non-zero digit are not significant ($0.0056$ has 2).\n   d. Trailing zeros after a decimal point in decimal numbers are significant ($5.400$ has 4).\n   e. In whole numbers without a decimal point, trailing zeros may or may not be significant depending on the instrument least count.\n3. Rounding Off Rules: If the discarded digit is $< 5$, drop it. If $> 5$, increase preceding digit by 1. If $= 5$, increase preceding digit if odd, leave unchanged if even.\n4. Arithmetic Rules:\n   a. Multiplication and Division: The final result must retain only as many significant figures as the least precise given factor.\n   b. Addition and Subtraction: The final result must retain only as many decimal places as the quantity having the smallest number of decimal places.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 1 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          }
        ]
