        "rawExercise": "A. 100 mm B. 200 mm C. 50 mm\nD. 10 mm\n2 Which of the following quantity can be measured using a micrometer?\nA. current\nB. force\nC. length\nD. mass\nThe instrument best measures the internal diameter of a pipe is\nMULTIPLE\nA. screw gauge\nC. meter rule\nB. vernier caliper\nD. measuring tape\nCE\nPUESTO\nWhich prefix represents a largest value?\nA. mega\nB. giga\nC. peta\nJaiqsa\nD. exa\n5 Which of the following is the smallest prefix?\nA. atto\nB. pico\nC. nano\nD. femto\n6 Which of the following numbers show one significant digit?\nA. 1.1\nB. 6.0\nC. 7.1\nD. 6 × 102\nWhich of the following numbers shows 4 significant digits?\nN\nA. 900.8\nS\nB. 4\nC. 5174.00 D. 0.0002\nA light year is distance traveled by light in one year. It travels about 9.460 x 1015 m. How many significant figures are in this number\nA. 6\nB. 2\n0.2 mm in units of meters is\nC. 3\nD. 4\nA. 0.0002m B. 2×10^m\nC. none\nD. both A and B\n10 KITAB UL MANAZIR is the name of book written by\nA. Yaqub Kindi\nC. Al Beruni\nB. Ibnal Haitham\nD. none\nPage\n26\nUnit - 1\nPhysical Quantities and Measurement\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\nGive a brief response to the following questions.\nHow technology is shaped by physics?\n2 Physics and biology are considered different branches of science, how\nphysics links with biology?\nWhy are measurements important?\nWhy area is a derived quantity?\nName any four derived units and write them as their base units.\n6 Why in physics we need to write in scientific notation?\nWhat is least count? How least count for vernier calliper and screw gauge are defined?\n8 How can we find the volume of a small pebble with the help of measuring\ncylinder?\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS\nGive an extended response to the following questions.\nDefine physics. How physics play a crucial role in science technology and society?\nWhat is SI? Name SI base quantities and their units.\nWhat are physical quantities? Distinguish between base and derived physical quantities.\nWhat is standard form or scientific notation?\nWhat are prefixes? Explain with examples.\nDescribe the construction and use for measurement of the following instruments:\na. Vernier Callipers\nb. Screw gauge\nWhat is meant by the significant figures of measurement? What are the main points to be kept in mind while determining the significant figures of a measurement?\nPage 27\nUnit 1\nPhysical Quantities and Measurement\nNUMERICAL QUESTIONS\n• Write the number in prefix to power of ten a. Mechanical nano-oscillators can detect a mass change as small as 10 kg. b. The nearest neutron star (a collapsed star made primarily of neutrons) is about 3.00 × 108 m away from Earth. c. Earth to sun distance is 149.6 million kilometere.\nAn angstrom (symbol Å) is a unit of length (commonly used in atomic physics), defined as 100 m which is of the order of the diameter of an atom. a. How many nanometers are in 1.0 angstrom?\nb. How many femtometers or fermis (the common unit of length in\nnuclear physics) are in 1.0 angstrom?\nc. How many angstroms are in 1.0 m?\nThe speed of light is c=299,792,458 m/s. a. Write this value in scientific notation.m\nb. Express the speed of light to\ni. five significant figures,\nor how 18 Welt\nto we\nii. three significant figures.\nExpress the following in terms of powers of 10.\na. 7 nanometre\nd. 43 picofarad\nb. 96 megawatt\ne. 2 millimetre.\nWrite the following numbers in standard form:\na.\nMass of Bacterial cell: 0.000,000,000,005 kg\nb. Diameter of Sun: 1,390,000,000 m\nc. 2 gigabite\nWEB LINKS\nhttp://www.introduction-to-physics.com/\nhttp://www.bipm.org/en/about-us/\nhttp://www.rapid tables.com/convert/number/scientific-notation-\nconverter.htm\nhttp://www.flippingphysics.com/significant-figures.html\nPage\n28\nUnit - 2\nKinematics",
