            "question": "What are Significant Figures? Explain all rules for identifying significant figures and the rules for rounding off in addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.",
            "answer": "1. Definition: In any physical measurement, significant figures are all accurately known digits plus the first doubtful (estimated) digit. They signify the precision of the measuring device.\n2. Rules for Identification:\n   a. All non-zero digits are significant ($4.23$ has 3).\n   b. Zeros between non-zero digits are significant ($2008$ has 4).\n   c. Leading zeros preceding the first non-zero digit are not significant ($0.0056$ has 2).\n   d. Trailing zeros after a decimal point in decimal numbers are significant ($5.400$ has 4).\n   e. In whole numbers without a decimal point, trailing zeros may or may not be significant depending on the instrument least count.\n3. Rounding Off Rules: If the discarded digit is $< 5$, drop it. If $> 5$, increase preceding digit by 1. If $= 5$, increase preceding digit if odd, leave unchanged if even.\n4. Arithmetic Rules:\n   a. Multiplication and Division: The final result must retain only as many significant figures as the least precise given factor.\n   b. Addition and Subtraction: The final result must retain only as many decimal places as the quantity having the smallest number of decimal places.",
