          "text": "We use the definitions of average velocity and acceleration to derive a set of valuable equations that relate x, a, v and t when a is constant, allowing us to determine any one of these variables if we know the others. We can then solve many interesting Problems.\n\nIn this discussion we chose the initial time to be zero t, = 0, and final time t = t such that At = t-tt-0=t. Also we chose initial position to be zero s = 0 and final position s, s, such that displacement As=s, - s1 =s-0 = s.\n\n=\n\nThe object has initial velocity v at point A, which changes uniformly to point B as v,. In the plot OA = DC represents initial velocity v, and DB represent final velocity v,. Here time t is represented by OD = AC=t. The slope of the line AB is\n\n82) J\n\nslope\n\n=\n\n195\n\nCB\n\nAC\n\nFigure 2.14) Equations of Motion\n\nvelocity v (ms)\n\nلنا\n\nE\n\na\n\nB\n\nat\n\nV1\n\nC\n\nA\n\nV1\n\ntime\n\n0\n\nD\n\nt (s)\n\nt\n\nThe slope of velocity-time graph gives acceleration a, since AC = t, therefore\n\nCB a=\n\nst\n\nor\n\nCB= at\n\nA.\n\nFirst Equation of Motion:AO bioxs961\n\nFirst equation of motion gives the relation of final velocity 'v,' in terms of initial velocity 'v,' and acceleration 'a' in time t. From the graph it is clear that\n\nDB = DC + CB\n\nLine DB represents, final\n\nDB = V\n\nvelocity v,\n\nputting these values in graph\n\nLine DC represents, initial\n\nequation we get\n\nDC = Vi\n\nvelocity v,\n\nLine segment CB, in terms\n\nVƒ = V; +at\n\n2.12\n\nCB= at\n\nof slope gives\n\nB.\n\ndisplacement\n\n=\n\nS\n\n1\n\n2\n\nSecond Equation of Motion:\n\nSecond equation of motion relates displacement s with initial velocity 'v' and acceleration 'a' in time 't'. As the area under velocity-time curve represents the displacement 's' as shown in figure 2.14. Therefore, the displacements of the\n\nobject is:\n\nS=(OA× AC) +x (AC x CB)\n\nArea of triangle\n\n▲ ACB\n\nFigure 2.15 Equations of Motion\n\nArea of rectangle.\n\nOACD\n\nLine segment AC represents, time t\n\nAC=t\n\nvelocity v (ms1)\n\nLine OA represents,\n\nB\n\ninitial velocity v1\n\nOA = Vi\n\nE\n\na\n\nLine segment CB, in\n\nterms of slope is\n\nCB = at\n\nputting values\n\n1\n\ns=v;xt += x(txat)\n\ntherefore s = vit+- at2\n\nixt+Ž\n\nt + 1/2 at2\n\n* = 10\n\nat\n\nA\n\nC\n\nt\n\nV1\n\ntime\n\nD\n\nt(s)\n\n2.13\n\nC.\n\nThird Equation of Motion:\n\nThird equation relate position s, velocity v (both final velocity v, and initial velocity v1), and constant acceleration a without including time t. The area under the curve can be calculated by taking the total area of the trapezium OABD. The velocity-time graph the area of a trapezoid OABD will give the total displacement\n\ns==(OA+DB) × OD\n\n.2\n\nPage\n\n50\n\nKinematics\n\nLine DB represents,\n\nFigure 2.16 Equations of Motion\n\nfinal velocity V,\n\nLine OA represents,\n\nDB = Vf\n\nvelocity v (ms')\n\ninitial velocity v1\n\nLine segment OD.\n\nOA = Vi\n\nB\n\nE\n\nrepresents, time t\n\nOD = t\n\nV1\n\nA\n\n1\n\nV1\n\nputting values\n\ns = {(V; +V;) xt\n\nO\n\ntime\n\nt\n\nD\n\nt (s)\n\nFrom first equation of motion\n\nVƒ = V; +at\n\nor\n\nat = Vf-Vi\n\nor\n\nt = Vƒ ~ Vi\n\na\n\n1\n\nS=\n\n1\n\nS=\n\nThe above equation becomes\n\n(Vf -Vi) (V; +Vƒ)׷\n\na\n\n·(V; +Vƒ)(Vƒ − V1)\n\n2a\n\nsince\n\n(a + b) x (a - b) = a2 - b2\n\ntherefore (V; +Vƒ)(Vƒ −V;) = v} −v}\n\nor S=\n\nv2-v?\n\n2a\n\n2.14\n\ntherefore 2as = v2-v?\n\nExample 2.5\n\nTIP\n\nkm/h to m/s\n\nTo convert km/h to m/s, divide by 3.6\n\nFigure 2.17 Trapezoid\n\n1,\n\nW\n\nThe area of a trapezoid is given by\n\nolmes\n\n1\n\nA = = (1, +12)x w\n\nwhich is simply the average of the\n\nparallel sides multiplied by the base.\n\nSPEEDING MOTORCYCLIST\n\nA motorcyclist starts from rest and moves with uniform acceleration of 1.9 m/s2 for 22 s. Find the (a) velocity and (b) distance covered?\n\nGIVEN:\n\ninitial velocity v1 = 0 m/s\n\nacceleration a = 1.9 m/s2\n\nTotal time t = 22 s\n\nREQUIRED:\n\na. Final velocity v, = ?\n\nb. Distance s = ?\n\nKinematics\n\na. To find final velocity we can use the first equation of motion\n\nputting values v; =0m/s +1.9 m/s2 x22s\n\nS\n\nO\n\nVƒ = V; +at\n\nL\n\ntherefore\n\nV1 = 41.8m/s\n\nAnswer\n\nT\n\nb. For the distance covered, we will use\n\nsecond equation of motion\n\n1\n\n0\n\nN\n\nputting values s=v,t+at2\n\n1\n\n1\n\ns = 0m/s × 225 + 2 × 1.9 m/s2 × (22 s)2\n\ntherefore S = 459.8m\n\nAnswer\n\nAssignment 2.5 CYCLIST MOTION\n\nEXTENSION EXERCISE 2.3\n\nThis problem is captioned speeding motorcyclist. Is 41.8 m/s safe speed for motorcycle ride? Also verify the distance covered in this problem by third equation of motion.\n\nA cyclist is moving with uniform acceleration of 1.2 m/s2. How much time will it require to change his velocity from 6 m/s to 12 m/s?\n\nExample 2.6 COMMERCIAL AIRCRAFT RUNWAY LENGTH\n\nA Boeing 777 aircraft takes off at 295 km/h after accelerating from rest at 2.80 m/s2. What is the minimum runway length required?\n\nS GIVEN:\n\ninitial velocity v, = 0 km/h = 0 m/s\n\nREQUIRED:\n\ndistance s = ?\n\nfinal velocity v1 =295 km/h 81.9 m/s\n\nL\n\nacceleration = 2.80 m/s2\n\nU\n\nConversion from km/h to m/s\n\nT\n\n295 km/h=\n\n295×103 m\n\n60×60 s\n\n295,000\n\n=81.9m/s\n\n3600 s\n\nΟ\n\nN Time is not given so we will use the third equation of motion\n\n2as = v2 -v2 or s =\n\nv} -v?\n\n(81.9ms-11)2-(0 ms-1)2\n\nputting values s =\n\n2a\n\n2×2.80 ms-2\n\nor\n\nS =\n\n6707.61m2/2\n\n5.6p 1\n\nS=1197.7875m\n\nAnswer\n\nThe units came out correctly as for displacement (meters) but we have to correct the number and express in three significant 19 of sub nou figures.\n\ns = 1200m s=1.20x103 m s=1.20 km\n\nA runway needs to be longer than 1.2 km as this was calculated minimum length to give a margin of safety. Most commercial airport runways are between 2 km and 4 km long. Assignment 2.6 BRAKING CAR\n\nEXTENSION EXERCISE 2.4\n\nAt landing, the 777 s velocity is about the same as at takeoff, and after touchdown its acceleration is normally between -2.0 m/s2 to -2.4 m/s2. How does this affect consideration of a safe runway length?\n\nOn Motorway M1, a car is moving at speed limit of 120 km/h. By applying brakes the car comes to rest after covering a distance of 30 m. What is the deceleration of the car?\n\nnarW\n\n2.5\n\nMOTION DUE TO GRAVITY:\n\nEveryone has observed the effect of Figure 2.18 Free Fall"
