          "text": "4.3 RESOLUTION OF FORCES\n\nThe process of splitting a force vector into two or more force vectors is called resolution of forces. The force vectors so obtained are called components.\n\nPOINT TO PONDER\n\nIs vector addition of more\n\nby head to tail rule? than two forces possible,\n\nThis process is opposite of addition of vectors, in principle force being a vector can be resolved into as many components as we wish. However If these components in to which a force vector is split are perpendicular to each other then such components are called rectangular components of vector. Resolving forces into rectangular components help us to analytically add forces rather than needing Figure 4.6 Rectangular Components a ruler, and protector. Part of the graphical technique is retained, because vectors are. still represented by arrows for easy visualization.\n\nAnalytical methods are more concise, accurate, and precise than graphical methods, which are limited by the accuracy with which a drawing can be made. Therefore, in physics, very often we\n\nX\n\n702\n\nΟΙ\n\nX\n\nF\n\nFx\n\nPage\n\n104\n\nTurning Effect of Forces\n\nneed to separate a vector into rectangular components. For example a ice block is being pulled by a boy using rope. We can think of force as tension in the rope. This single force F can be resolved into two components - one directed rightward along x-axis (F) and the other directed upwards along y-axis (F,) as shown in figure 4.6.\n\nConsider a force F in the Cartesian Figure 4.7 Rectangular Components coordinate System, .represented by the\n\nline OP, making an angle e as shown in the figure 4.7.\n\nDraw perpendiculars from point P on x-axis and y-axis which meets the axis at. points Q and S respectively. Put arrow head from the direction of O towards Q and S such that they represent vectors as F (OQ & SP) and F, (OS & QP), becomes the rectangular components of vector F (OP).\n\nBy head to tail rule of vector addition, we know that force F is the vector sum of F, and F,, mathematically\n\nF =Ēx + Fy\n\n42\n\nComponents represented in terms of force: Consider a triangle A OPQ, without taking the sides as vectors. This forms a right angle triangle for which we have.\n\nBase\n\nOQ\n\ncose\n\nhyp\n\nOP\n\nFx\n\nor\n\ncose =\n\nF\n\ntherefore\n\nsimilarly\n\nsine =\n\nor\n\nFx = F cose\n\nsine =\n\nPerp QP\n\nhyp\n\nFy\n\nF\n\n=\n\nOP\n\nx-\n\nS\n\nF\n\nتمام\n\n0\n\nعام\n\nP\n\nQ\n\nth\"\n\nTABLE 4.11 ANGLE VALUES\n\nӨ\n\nsin 0\n\ncos 0\n\ntan 0\n\n0°\n\n0.000\n\n1.000\n\n0.000\n\n30°\n\n0.500\n\n0.866\n\n0.577\n\n45° 0.707 .0.707\n\n1.000\n\n9\n\n60°\n\n0.866\n\n0.500\n\n1.732\n\n90° 1.000\n\n0.000\n\ninfinite\n\n4.3\n\nPage\n\n105\n\ntherefore\n\nFy = F sine\n\n4.4\n\nP\n\nThis means that we can calculate the components analytically just by knowing the value of force and the angle.\n\nForce represented in terms of its Components: Since triangle A OPQ, form a right angle triangle, therefore we can use Pythagorus theorem, which\n\nstates that\n\n(hyp)2 = (base)2 +(perp)2.\n\nQ..\n\nF\n\nFy\n\n0\n\nFx\n\n-\n\nQ...\n\n√(hyp)2 = √(base)2 + (perp)2\n\ntaking square root on both sides\n\nsuch that\n\nhyp = √(base)2 +(perp)2\n\nor:\n\nOP = √(0Q)2 + (QP)2\n\ntherefore\n\nF = √F2 + F2\n\n4.5\n\nThe magnitude of vector can now be determined if the values of the magnitudes of components are known. To determine the direction in right angle triangle A OPQ, we have tan 8 as\n\ntane\n\nperp QP base OQ\n\nFy\n\nor.\n\ntane\n\nF\n\ntherefore\n\n0 = tan-1 Fy\n\n4.6\n\nFx\n\nExample 4.2\n\nPULLING A BOX ON FLOOR\n\nDivia is pulling a box on the floor win a force of 20 N making an angle of 60° with the horizontal. Find the horizontal and vertical components of this force.\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nGIVEN\n\nForce F= 20.0 N\n\nAngle = 60°\n\n(a) Horizontal component of force F1 = ?\n\n(b) Vertical component of force F1 = ?\n\nPage\n\n106\n\nTurning Effect of Forces\n\n(a) Horizontal component of force F, is the force that is involved in moving the box along\n\nthe floor, Using equation 4.3\n\nFx = F cose\n\nputting values\n\nFx = 20 N cos 60°\n\ntherefore\n\nFx = 10N\n\nAnswer\n\nF = 20.0 N\n\n60°\n\n0\n\n(a) For vertical component of force F, we will use equation 4.4\n\nF1 = F sine\n\nputting values\n\ntherefore\n\nFy = 20N sin 60°\n\nFy=17.3N- Answer\n\nTo check the answer, we can convert the components back into magnitude and direction.\n\nF = √F }2 + F }\n\nputting values\n\nor\n\ntherefore\n\nF = = √(10)2+(17.3)2\n\nF= √100+299.3\n\n1 F =19.9N=20N\n\nThe magnitude is nearly equal to 20 N, therefore our calculations are correct, now to check the angle\n\n0 = tan-11y\n\niFy\n\nFx\n\nputting values\n\nor\n\ntherefore\n\n0 = tan-117.3\n\n10\n\n0 = tan-11.73\n\n0 = 60°\n\nAs the angle is equal to 60°, therefore our calculations are correct.\n\nAssignment 4.2 TILLING GARDEN\n\nWhile tilling your garden, you exert a force on the handles of the tiller that has components F1 = 85 N and F, = 13 N. The x-axis is horizontal and the y-axis points up. What are the magnitude and direction of this force?\n\nPage\n\n107\n\nTurning Effect of Forces"
