            "pressure",
            "An instrument that is commonly used to measure atmospheric pressure is the mercury barometer. Italian mathematician Evangelista Torricelli (1608-1647) invented it in 1643. Figure 7.2 shows a Torricellian barometer. A long tube that is open at one end and closed at the other is filled with mercury and then inverted into a dish of mercury. Once the tube is inverted, the mercury does not empty into the bowl. Instead, the atmosphere exerts a pressure on the mercury in the bowl. This atmospheric pressure pushes the mercury in the tube to some height 'h' above the bowl. In this way, the force exerted on the bowl of mercury by the atmosphere is equal to the weight of the column of mercury",
            "h",
            "h",
            "in the tube. Any change in the height 'h' A mercury barometer. If there is no air in",
