          "text": "7.5 PASCAL'S PRINCIPLE\n\npressure\n\nexerts a force on the air inside the tyre. The air responds by pushing not only When you pump a bicycle tyre, you apply a force on the pump that in turn against the pump but also against the walls of the tyre. As a result, the pressure increases by an equal amount throughout the tyre. In general, if the pressure in a fluid is increased at any point in a container (such as at the valve of the tyre), the pressure increases at all points inside the container by exactly the same amount. Blaise Pascal (1623-1662) noticed this fact in what is now called Pascal's principle\n\n(or Pascal's law):\n\nPage\n\n179\n\nAn external pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted unchange\n\nto every point within the fluid.\n\nClassic example of Pascal's principle at work is the hydraulic lift, which\n\nshown schematically in figure 7.3. Here we see two cylinders, one of cross- sectional area A, the other of cross-sectional area A2 > A,. The cylinders, each of which is fitted with a piston, are connected by a tube and filled with a suitable fluid usually oil called Hydraulic fluid. Initially the pistons are at the same level and exposed to the atmosphere.\n\nFigure 7.3\n\nHYDRAULIC LIFT\n\nF.\n\npiston (1)\n\nd\n\nΑπ\n\npiston\n\n(2)\n\nHydraulic Fluid\n\nF2\n\nNow, suppose we push down on piston (1) with the force F1. The pressure?, exerted by this piston is\n\nF1\n\nP1\n\nA1\n\nSimilarly, the pressure on the\n\npiston (2) lifting vehicle is P2, which can\n\nbe written as\n\nF2\n\nP2\n\n2\n\nA2\n\n3\n\nBy pascal's principle\n\nP2 = P1\n\nputting values from equation 1 and equation 2 in equation 3, we get\n\nF2 A2\n\n=\n\n=\n\nor\n\nF2\n\nor\n\nF2 =\n\nF1 A1\n\nA2 x F1 A1\n\nA2 F1\n\n7.3\n\nDepending on the ratio A,/A, the force F, can be as large as required. To be\n\nPage\n\n180\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nspecific, let's assume that A, is 100 times greater than A,. Then, by pushing down on piston 1 with a force F, we push upward on piston 2 with a force of F2 = 100 F,. Our force has been magnified 100 times! Hence a relatively small effort can be used to overcome a much larger load.\n\nAPPLICATION\n\nHYDRAULIC BRAKES OPERATION\n\nHydraulic car brakes are shown in Figure. When the brake pedal is\n\npushed, the piston in the master cylinder exerts a force on the brake fluid and the resulting pressure is transmitted equally to eight other pistons. These force the brake shoes or pads against the wheels and stop the car.\n\nbrake drum\n\nbrake\n\npedal\n\nbrake\n\nshoe\n\nmaster cylinder\n\nreturn\n\nspring\n\npistons\n\nbrake oil"
