        "7.1 Kinetic Molecular Model of Matter\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nLook around, everything, from our body to the air we breath is matter. The features of a substance and how it behave are called its properties. Therefore it is important to know about the properties of matter. Knowing about properties of matter helps us to understand what ice cube rain and steam have in common? What is the impact of amount of substance in a body? How matter behaves when forces are applied? Questions like these can be answered after reading this\n\nchapter.\n\nWe will see how solids respond to external forces, and how fluids liquids and gases develop pressure that is responsible for such phenomena as buoyancy\n\nand fluid flow.\n\n7.1\n\nKINETIC MOLECULAR MODEL OF MATTER\n\nIt is well established by chemical and physical evidence that matter is composed of molecules. Matter has three states solids, liquids and gases. These three states of matter are explained on the basis of Kinetic Molecular Theory. In kinetic molecular model, it is assumed that:\n\nSolids:\n\n1. Solids are made up of molecules which are arranged closely in a fixed pattern.\n\n2. Molecules in solids vibrate about their mean positions.\n\n3. The attractive forces between the molecules are strong.\n\nLiquids:\n\n1. Liquids are also made up of molecules which are close together.\n\n2. The pattern of molecules is not fixed and does not extend far. The molecules in\n\na pattern keep changing their position.\n\n3. Molecules are able to move about, which means that a liquid is able to change\n\nits shape and can adopt the shape of the container.\n\n4. The attractive forces between the molecules of liquid is less than the solid..\n\nGases:\n\n1. Agas is made up of molecules which are in constant random motion.\n\n2. The distance between molecules is larger as compared to the size of the\n\nmolecules.\n\n3. The molecules are constantly colliding elastically with each other and with the\n\nwalls of the container.\n\n4. Forces between molecules are negligible, except during collisions.\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nBecause liquids and gases do not maintain a fixed shape, they both have the ability to flow. They are thus also referred collectively as fluids.\n\n7.1\n\nKINETIC MOLECULAR MODEL OF MATTER\n\nSolid molecules vibrate in fixed positions.\n\nLiquid molecules flow over each other.\n\nGas molecules move rapidly in all directions and collide.\n\nPLASMA:\n\nThere is another state of matter, the fourth state, called Plasma. It consists of free electrons and atoms from which the electrons have been removed. Plasma exists in the sun, where thermonuclear reactions take place at very high temperatures.",
        "7.2 Density\n\n7.2 DENSITY\n\nDensity of a substance is defined\n\nas the mass of substance per unit volume. For example iron is denser than air, because iron has more mass in the same volume as compared to air. Conversely for same mass of iron and air, volume of iron will be less than air. Symbolically density is denoted by 'p' (called Rho). For an object of mass 'm' and volume 'V', density is given as\n\nTABLE 7.1 DENSITIES\n\nMaterial\n\niron\n\nDensity (kg/m3)\n\n7900\n\ngold\n\n19300\n\nice\n\n920\n\npolythene\n\n900\n\npetrol\n\n800\n\npure water\n\n1000\n\n13600\n\nmercury\n\nair\n\n1.3\n\n2.0\n\nm\n\n7.1\n\nV\n\ncarbon dioxide\n\nHence the S.I. unit of density is kg m3. Some common density values, are\n\ngiven in table 7.1.\n\nPage\n\n173\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nExample 7.1\n\nDENSITY OF METAL\n\nA rectangular metal piece has length 0.3 m, width 0.2 m and height 0.1 m. If its\n\nmass is 47.4 kg. Calculate its density.\n\nS GIVEN:\n\nO Mass of metal piece m = 47.4 kg\n\nL Volume V = length x width x height\n\nU\n\n= 0.3 m x 0.2 m x 0.1 m = 0.006 m\n\nREQUIRED:\n\ndensity p =?\n\nm\n\nBy definition of density' p =\n\nV\n\n0\n\n47.4kg\n\nputting valties\n\nΡ\n\n0.006 m3\n\nN\n\ntherefore 24th\n\np = 7900 kg m-3\n\nAnswer\n\nFrom the table 7.1 we can see that it is density of iron, therefore the metal piece under study is that of iron.\n\nAssignment 7.1 DENSITY OF DIAMOND\n\nA diamond has a volume of 0.00002 m3, its mass is measured\n\nas 0.072 kg. Calculate its density.",
        "7.3 Pressure\n\n7.3 PRESSURE\n\nif\n\nQUIZ?\n\nSMAE VOLUME OBJECTS\n\nPressure is defined as force per unit area. Pressure is represented by letter 'P', force 'F' is applied on area 'A', the pressure is\n\nF\n\n7.2\n\nA\n\nWhich is a heavier, a cubic metre of steel or a cubic metre of wood? Why?\n\nTID-BIT\n\nTHUMB PIN\n\nThe SI unit of pressure is the newton per\n\nsquare meter (N/m2), which is given a special\n\nname, the pascal (Pa).\n\n1N 1Pa =\n\n1m2\n\nAssuming same force is applied at both ends of this thumb- pin, on which\n\nside pressure is greater?\n\nPage\n\n174\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nACTIVITY PRESSURE AND PRICKING\n\nPOINT TO PONDER HEELES\n\nPressure is increased if the force applied to a given area is increased, or if a given force is applied to a smaller area. For example, if you press your finger against a balloon, not much happens-your finger causes a small indentation. On the other hand, if you push a needle against the balloon with the same force, you get an explosive pop. The difference is that the same force applied to the small area of a needle tip causes a large enough pressure to rupture the balloon.\n\nExample 7.2\n\nHave you ever wonderd why getting steped on by heeled shoe hurts more than getting stepped by flat one?\n\nSince the pressure depends upon the amount of force acting on the surface and size of the surface. Therefore for same force smaller surface exerts greater pressure. For the same reason the spiked shoes are painful to wear, as it concentrate your body weight (force) on the ball of foot.\n\nWEIGHT AS PRESSURE ON GROUND\n\nA 40 kg girl's two feet cover an area of 500 cm2 (a) Determine the pressure exerted by the two feet on the ground. (b) If the girl stands on one foot, what will be the pressure under that foot?.\n\nGIVEN:\n\nForce F = Weight W = mg = 40 kg x 9.8 m s2 = 392 N\n\nArea A=500 cm2 = 500 x (102)2 m2 = 500 x 10 m2 = 0.05 m2\n\n(a) Assume the girl is at rest. Then she exerts\n\na force equal to her weight mg on the ground\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nPressure P = ?\n\nEXTENSION EXERCISE 7.1\n\nwhere her feet contact it. Therefore by what will be pressure if she\n\ndefinition of pressure\n\nputting values\n\nP\n\nF\n\nA\n\nP =\n\n392 N 0.05m2\n\nis wearing heeled shoe with an area of tip 0.0001 m2, and stand with all her weight on\n\nthat tip?\n\nPage\n\n175\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nor\n\nP = 7840N m2\n\nor\n\nP = 7.8×103N m2\n\nor\n\nP = 7.8 kPa\n\nAnswer\n\n(b) If the person stands on one foot, the force is still equal to the person's weight, but the area will be half as much, so the pressure will be twice as\n\nmuch\n\nP=15.6 kPa\n\nAssignment 7.2 CONCRETE BLOCK\n\nAnswer\n\nA block of concrete weighs 900 N and its base is a square of side 3 m. What pressure does the block exert on the ground?",
        "7.4 Atmospheric Pressure\n\n7.4 ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE\n\nPressure is particularly useful for dealing with fluids. It is an experimental observation that a fluid exerts pressure in every direction. This is well known to swimmers and divers who feel the water pressure on all parts of their bodies. A person need not be under water to experience the effects of pressure. Walking about on land, we are at the bottom of the earth's atmosphere, which is a fluid and pushes inward on our bodies just like the water in a swimming pool.\n\nThe pressure that atmosphere exerts is called atmospheric pressure. The pressure of the air at a given place varies slightly according to the weather. At sea level, the pressure of the atmosphere on average is 1.013 × 105 Nm2 (or 1.013x 103 Pa).This value lets us to define a commonly used unit of pressure, the atmosphere (abbreviated atm), such that 1 atm = 1.013 × 10' Pa.\n\nAnother unit of pressure sometimes used (in meteorology and on weather\n\nmaps) is the bar, which is defined as 1 bar = 1.000 × 105 Pa\n\naround us exerts a force in all directions and is opposed by an equal pressure inside Thus standard atmospheric pressure is slightly more than 1 bar. The air our body, thus we are generally unaware of it. However, when the air is pumped out of a sealed can, atmospheric pressure produces an inward force that is unopposed. The resulting collapse of the can illustrates the pressure that is all\n\nthinner air. The thinner air causes breathing difficulties due to the lower level of The climbers at high altitudes encounter lower atmospheric pressure due to the\n\naround us.\n\noxygen.\n\nACTIVITY\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nUSING ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE TO CRUSH CAN\n\nFigure shows a thin-walled metal can, attached to a vacuum pump. Before the air was pumped out, the pressure inside the can is equal to that outside (atmospheric) pressure. As the air is pumped out, a partial vacuum of very low pressure forms inside the can and immediately the external atmospheric pressure crushes the can. It will only happen if the material of the can is thin or flexible.\n\n7.4.1 MEASUREMENT OF ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE\n\nFigure 7.2 BAROMETER\n\nNormal atmospheric\n\npressure\n\nHigher atmospheric\n\npressure\n\nAn instrument that is commonly used to measure atmospheric pressure is the mercury barometer. Italian mathematician Evangelista Torricelli (1608-1647) invented it in 1643. Figure 7.2 shows a Torricellian barometer. A long tube that is open at one end and closed at the other is filled with mercury and then inverted into a dish of mercury. Once the tube is inverted, the mercury does not empty into the bowl. Instead, the atmosphere exerts a pressure on the mercury in the bowl. This atmospheric pressure pushes the mercury in the tube to some height 'h' above the bowl. In this way, the force exerted on the bowl of mercury by the atmosphere is equal to the weight of the column of mercury\n\nh\n\nh\n\nin the tube. Any change in the height 'h' A mercury barometer. If there is no air in\n\nof the column of mercury means that the atmosphere's pressure has changed. At sea level, the atmosphere will push down on a pool of mercury and make it rise up in a tube to a height of approximately 760 mm.\n\nthe tube, the air pressure on the mercury in the bowl forces the mercury to rise up the tube. The pressure at the bottom of the tube equals the air pressure. The higher the air pressure, the higher the column of mercury.\n\nPage\n\n177\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nIs it possible to build a barometer with water rather than mercury? Water is less dense (less heavy, in effect) than mercury so air pressure will lifta certain volume of water much higher up a tube than the same volume of mercury. In other words, if we use water, we would need a really tall tube and\n\nour barometer will be so enormous as to be impractical.\n\n7.4.2 ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE AND WEATHER\n\nBarometers kept in the same place at the same height above sea level shows some variation in atmospheric pressure from day to day. These pressure variations are shown on weather maps. The lines in the map joining all those places with the same atmospheric pressure are called isobars. The unit for pressure used in weather maps is the millibar (m bar).\n\n1000 mbar = 1 bar\n\n1 bar = 100 kPa, nearly equal to normal atmospheric pressure. Usually the range of atmospheric pressure varies from the very\n\nSea level Pressure Anomaly (mb) May 2011\n\nRussia\n\nGreent\n\nNorth Amenca\n\nP NSDC\n\nhigh pressure of 1040 mbar to as low as 950 mbar. Winds move from high pressure regions to low pressure regions. In the northern hemisphere, the wind moves anticlockwise around areas of low pressure and clockwise around areas of high pressure. The strength of the wind is determined by the pressure gradient. From the weather map, when the isobars are packed closely together, it indicates a high pressure gradient. This means that strong winds may result.\n\nAPPLICATION\n\nDRINKING THROUGH STRAW\n\nThe action of sucking increases the volume of lungs, thereby reducing the air pressure in the lungs and the mouth. The atmospheric pressure acting on the surface of the liquid will then be greater than the pressure in the mouth, thus forcing the liquid to rise up the straw into the mouth.\n\natmospheric pressure\n\nPressure less than atmospheric\n\npressure\n\nLiquid Pushed up the straw\n\nPage\n\n178\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nWORKING OF VACUUM CLEANER\n\nAPPLICATION\n\nVacuum cleaner consists of an\n\nelectric motor with fan, filter bag (dirt bag) and a tube. When the electric motor is switched on the fan rotates in such a way it sucks the air inside container and sends it out through exhaust, creating a partial vacuum inside the container. By doing so, a low pressure region is created inside the container.\n\nexhaust\n\nwith fan\n\nlow pressure\n\nelectric motor\n\nA\n\ntube\n\nDust\n\natmospheric pressure\n\nAs we already know materials move from high pressure region to low pressure region, here, surrounding air moves from outside the container via the tube to inside the container. While surrounding air moves inside with more force, it gathers all the dirt and dust that comes across its way, thus pulling them inside the container. These dust and dirt particles are filtered and stored in the filter bag, which will be disposed later.\n\nAPPLICATION\n\nPULLING LIQUID UP THE SYRINGE\n\nPressure less than atmospheric pressure\n\nTo draw liquid into the syringe, as shown in Figure, the piston of the syringe is drawn back upwards. This decreases the pressure within the cylinder. Atmospheric pressure acting on atmospheric the surface of the liquid drives the liquid into the cylinder through the nozzle of the syringe. Thus atomospheric pressure helps the fluid to\n\nlift up the cylinder.",
        "7.5 Pascal's Principle\n\n7.5 PASCAL'S PRINCIPLE\n\npressure\n\nexerts a force on the air inside the tyre. The air responds by pushing not only When you pump a bicycle tyre, you apply a force on the pump that in turn against the pump but also against the walls of the tyre. As a result, the pressure increases by an equal amount throughout the tyre. In general, if the pressure in a fluid is increased at any point in a container (such as at the valve of the tyre), the pressure increases at all points inside the container by exactly the same amount. Blaise Pascal (1623-1662) noticed this fact in what is now called Pascal's principle\n\n(or Pascal's law):\n\nPage\n\n179\n\nAn external pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted unchange\n\nto every point within the fluid.\n\nClassic example of Pascal's principle at work is the hydraulic lift, which\n\nshown schematically in figure 7.3. Here we see two cylinders, one of cross- sectional area A, the other of cross-sectional area A2 > A,. The cylinders, each of which is fitted with a piston, are connected by a tube and filled with a suitable fluid usually oil called Hydraulic fluid. Initially the pistons are at the same level and exposed to the atmosphere.\n\nFigure 7.3\n\nHYDRAULIC LIFT\n\nF.\n\npiston (1)\n\nd\n\nΑπ\n\npiston\n\n(2)\n\nHydraulic Fluid\n\nF2\n\nNow, suppose we push down on piston (1) with the force F1. The pressure?, exerted by this piston is\n\nF1\n\nP1\n\nA1\n\nSimilarly, the pressure on the\n\npiston (2) lifting vehicle is P2, which can\n\nbe written as\n\nF2\n\nP2\n\n2\n\nA2\n\n3\n\nBy pascal's principle\n\nP2 = P1\n\nputting values from equation 1 and equation 2 in equation 3, we get\n\nF2 A2\n\n=\n\n=\n\nor\n\nF2\n\nor\n\nF2 =\n\nF1 A1\n\nA2 x F1 A1\n\nA2 F1\n\n7.3\n\nDepending on the ratio A,/A, the force F, can be as large as required. To be\n\nPage\n\n180\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nspecific, let's assume that A, is 100 times greater than A,. Then, by pushing down on piston 1 with a force F, we push upward on piston 2 with a force of F2 = 100 F,. Our force has been magnified 100 times! Hence a relatively small effort can be used to overcome a much larger load.\n\nAPPLICATION\n\nHYDRAULIC BRAKES OPERATION\n\nHydraulic car brakes are shown in Figure. When the brake pedal is\n\npushed, the piston in the master cylinder exerts a force on the brake fluid and the resulting pressure is transmitted equally to eight other pistons. These force the brake shoes or pads against the wheels and stop the car.\n\nbrake drum\n\nbrake\n\npedal\n\nbrake\n\nshoe\n\nmaster cylinder\n\nreturn\n\nspring\n\npistons\n\nbrake oil",
        "7.6 Liquid Pressure\n\n7.6 LIQUID PRESSURE\n\nThe liquid is incompressible therefore the pressure increases directly below the surface of the liquid. In figure 7.4 a disc is placed at the bottom of a tank in fluid. The force Facting on the disc is the weight W of the cylindrical column of liquid of mass m.\n\nF=W = mg\n\nFor the fluid of density p, the mass m of the fluid above the disk, with\n\nvolume Vis\n\nor\n\nV\n\nm = pV\n\n2\n\nSince the fluid forms a cylindrical volume V shown by dotted lines in the figure which has height h and area of cross section A. Therefore\n\nV = Ah\n\nputting equation 3 in equation 2, we\n\nFigure 7.4 LIQUID PRESSURE\n\nDisk\n\nד\n\nh\n\nget\n\nm = p Ah\n\nA\n\nPage\n\n181\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nputting equation 4 in equation 1, we get\n\nF = pAhg\n\nSince pressure is defined as\n\n(5)\n\nF P =\n\n6\n\nA\n\nINFORMATION\n\nh\n\n1\n\nA\n\nB C\n\nD\n\nputting value of force from equation 5 in\n\nequation 6 we get\n\np Ahg P =\n\nA\n\ntherefore P = phg\n\n7.4\n\nPROPERTIES OF LIQUIDS AND PRESSURE\n\nSince points A, B, C, and D are at the same distance h beneath the liquid surface, the pressure at each of them is the same.\n\n1) Pressure in a liquid increases with depth because the further down you go, the greater the weight of liquid above. As can be seen in Figure. A water spurts out fastest and furthest from the\n\nlowest hole.\n\n2) Pressure at one depth acts equally in all directions.\n\nThe can of water in Figure has similar holes all round it at the same level. Water comes out equally fast and spurts equally far from each hole. Hence the pressure exerted by the water at this depth is the same in all directions.\n\nA\n\nPage\n\n182\n\nProperties of Matter\n\n(3) A liquid finds its own level. In figure the liquid is at the same level in each tube and confirms that the pressure at the bottom of a liquid depends only on the vertical depth of the liquid and not on the tube width or\n\nshape.\n\nExample 7.3\n\nLIMITS ON SUBMARINE DEPTH\n\n0\n\nAsubmarine is constructed so that it can safely withstand a pressure of 1.6 x 10' Pa. How deep may this submarine descend in the ocean if the average density of seawater is 1025 kg/m3?\n\nS GIVEN:\n\n0 Pressure P=1.6 x 10' Pa = 1.6 x 10' kg m's2\n\nDensity=1025 kg/m3\n\nAcceleration due to gravity g = 9.8 ms2\n\n(a) The maximum depth a submarine can go down\n\ncan be measured by equation\n\nREQUIRED:\n\ndepth = height h = ?\n\nN\n\nP = phg\n\nP\n\nor\n\nh=\n\nPg\n\nputting values\n\nh=\n\n1.6×107 kgm ́1s2\n\n1025 kgm3 x 9.8 ms\n\ntherefore h=1.6 × 103m = 1.6 km\n\nAnswer\n\nAssignment 7.3 PRESSURE AT 100 m DEPTH OF WATER What is the pressure at a depth of 100 m below the surface of water?\n\nPage\n\n183\n\nProperties of Matter",
        "7.7 Upthrust and Buoyancy\n\n7.7 UPTHRUST AND BUOYANCY\n\nWhen an object is immersed Figure 7.5 UPTHRUST\n\nin a fluid, the pressure on the lower surface of the object is higher than the pressure on the upper surface. The difference in pressures leads to an upward net force acting on the object due to the fluid pressure called up-thurst or buoyant force and phenomena is called buoyancy.\n\n-If you try to push a piece of cork underwater, you feel the buoyant force pushing the cork back\n\nup.",
        "7.8 Archimedes' Principle\n\n7.8 ARCHIMEDES PRINCIPLE\n\nThe buoyant\n\nNet force F. of the fluid on the\n\ncork is the buoyant force F,\n\nforce arises because the fluid pressure at the bottom Increasing of the cylinder is larger than at the top.\n\nPressure\n\nF\n\nபட\n\nF\n\ndown\n\nFup> Fdown because pressure is greater at bottom of beaker, hence the fluid exerts a net upward force\n\nWhy do some things float while others do not? Is floating determined by the weight of the object? A large ship floats, but a small pebble quickly sinks. Clearly, it is not a matter of the total weight of the object. The density of the object may be the key. Objects that are denser than the fluid will sink while those less dense will float. The complete answer to the question of why things sink or float is found in Archimedes' principle, which is stated as\n\nThe buoyant force acting on an object fully or partially submerged in a\n\nfluid is equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the object.\n\nEveryone has experienced Archimedes' principle. For example, it is\n\nrelatively easy to lift someone in a swimming pool.\n\nImagine that you submerge a brick in a container of water, as shown in Figure 7.6. A spout on the side of the container at the water's surface allows water to flow out of the container. As the brick sinks, the water level rises and water flows through the spout into a smaller container. The total volume of water that collects in the smaller container is the displaced volume of water from the large container. The displaced volume of water is equal to the volume of the portion of\n\nPage\n\n184\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nthe brick that is underwater. Figure 7.6 ARCHIMEDES' PRINCIPLE\n\na. A brick is being lowered into a container of water.\n\nb. The brick displaces water, causing the water to flow into a smaller container.\n\nc. When the brick is completely submerged, the volume of the displaced water is equal to the volume of the brick.\n\nThe magnitude of the buoyant force F, acting on the brick at any given time can be calculated by using Archimedes' principle. Any object completely or partially submerged in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal in magnitude to the weight of the fluid displaced by the object.\n\nF=W (displaced fluid) = m,g\n\nmagnitude of buoyant force = weight of fluid displaced According to Archimedes Figure 7.7 FLOAT OR SINK\n\nprinciple, every object\n\nexperiences a buoyant force. The buoyant force will decrease the weight of the object which is termed as apparent loss of weight. Whether it floats or sinks depends on the object's density relative to the fluid. When we push a wood block underwater and you will feel the upward buoyant force as in figure 7.7(a). By Archimedes principle, the buoyant force equals the weight of water displaced; since water is denser than wood, the buoyant force is greater than the wood's weight, and that is why wood\n\n(a)\n\nwood 4F\n\ncoin\n\nW\n\n(b)\n\nFe\n\nF\n\nnet\n\nW\n\nF1 = weight of fluid displaced by wood. Water is denser than\n\nwood Pwater > Pwood so block's weight W is less than F, and the net force is upward.\n\nF1 = weight of fluid displaced by coin.\n\nCoin is denser than\n\nwater Pwater Pcoin\n\nso coin's weight Wis\n\ngreater than F., and the net force is downward.\n\nPage\n\n185\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nfloats. Now when we submerge a coin figure 7.7(b). It is denser than water, so the coin's weight is greater than the weight of the displaced water. Hence the coin's\n\nweight is greater than the upward buoyant force, and it sinks.\n\nWhether an object will float or sink depends on the net force acting on it.\n\nThis net force can be calculated as follows:\n\nFnet = FB-W (object)\n\nNow we can apply Archimedes' principle, using 'm,' to represent the mass of the submerged object and 'm,' as mass of fluid 'g' is acceleration due to gravity.\n\nFnet = mg - mg\n\nRemember that 'm = pV', so the expression can be rewritten as follows:\n\nFnet = PV18-Povog or Fnet = (PV-PVs\n\nA simple relationship between the weight 'W' of a submerged object of mass m。 and density p, and the buoyant force 'F' on the object by fluid displaced of mass m, and density p, can be found by considering their ratio as follows:\n\nW\n\n=\n\nm\n\nFg mg\n\nW\n\nor\n\n=\n\nf\n\nm。\n\nΟ\n\nF met grou\n\nB\n\nfor submerged object equal volume is displaced therefore\n\ntherefore\n\nV1 = V1 = V m1 = p_V\n\nB\n\n70\n\nand\n\nm1 = p,V\n\nputting these values in equation 1, we get\n\nW\n\nFB\n\n=\n\nPY\n\nΡ\n\nP,V\n\nW\n\nP。\n\nor\n\n=\n\n7.5\n\nFB P\n\n091800\n\nLAB WORK\n\nTo find the density of a body heavier than water by Archimedes Principle.\n\nExample 7.4 IS CROWN MADE OF PURE GOLD?\n\nA girl purchases a \"gold\" crown at a flea market. After she gets home, she hangs the crown from a scale and finds its weight to be 7.84 N. She then weighs the crown while it is\n\nimmersed in water and the scale reads 6.86 N. Is the crown made of pure gold?\n\nPage\n\n186\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nGIVEN:\n\n0 Weight in air W1 = 7.84 N\n\nWeight in water W, = 6.86 N\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nDensity of crown p。 = ?\n\nDensity of water p1 = 1.00 × 103 kg/m3\n\nThe apparent weight of the crown in water W, is equal to the weight in air W minus the buoyant force F, exerted by water i.e. W1 = W-F, which means that\n\nF-W = W\n\nA\n\nF=7.84N-6.86N\n\nFg= 0.98N\n\nThe relationship between the weight 'W' and\n\nthe buoyant force 'F,' is\n\n6.86 N\n\n7.84 N\n\nW P。\n\nFB\n\nP,\n\nRearrange the equation to isolate the unknown:\n\nPo\n\n=\n\n=\n\nW\n\nX\n\nFR 7.84N\n\nputting values Po 0.98 N\n\ntherefore P.\n\nx1x103 kg/m3\n\nAnswer\n\n= 8×103 kg/m3\n\nIn Air\n\nIn Water\n\nFrom Table 7.1, the density of gold is 19.3 x 10' kg/m3. As 8.0 x 10' kg/m3 is quite less than 19.3 x 103 kg/m3, the crown cannot be pure gold.\n\nAssignment 7.4 DENSITY OF ROCK\n\nAgeologist finds that a Moon rock whose weight is 90.9 N has an apparent weight of 60.6 N when submerged in water. What is the density of the rock?\n\nACTIVITY\n\nWATER AND OIL\n\nPour some water into a clear glass. Then pour in some cooking oil. Which liquid is less dense?\n\nPage\n\n187\n\nAPPLICATION\n\nHYDROMETER\n\nA hydrometer is an instrument that measures the -the ratio of the density of the liquid to the density of water. This is a glass tube with a scale on the side weighted at the bottom with lead shot. The hydrometer floats in the liquid and if the liquid is dense the hydrometer does not need to sink very low to displace its own weight of liquid. If the liquid has a lower density it will sink much deeper.\n\nbulb\n\ncar battery acid\n\nhydrometer\n\nlow density high density liquid liquid\n\nHydrometers are calibrated for different uses, such as a lactometer for measuring the density (creaminess) of milk, a saccharometer for measuring the density of sugar in a liquid, or an alcoholometer for measuring higher levels of alcohol in spirits.\n\nA balloon, filled with helium (a gas lighter than air) will be pushed upwards by the denser air when it is released. This is because upthrust is greater than the total downward weight of the helium and the material of the balloon.\n\nPOINT TO PONDER\n\nHellum gas\n\nballoon Air is denser than\n\nhelium and material of balloon therefore an upthrust is applied on balloon\n\nFluids of different A steel ball floats in\n\ndensities float on\n\ntop of each other.\n\nmercury because\n\nthe density of\n\nmercury is greater than that of steel.\n\nProperties of Matter",
        "7.9 Elasticity\n\n7.9 ELASTICITY\n\nIn everyday conversation if someone speaks Figure 7.8 to you about an elastic body, you probably immediately think of a rubber band. A rubber band yields a great deal to a distorting force, and yet it returns to its original length after the distorting\n\nforce is removed\n\nThe property of solid materials to return to their original shape and size after removal of deforming force is called elasticity.\n\nWhen an archer shoots an arrow, he bends the bow which springs back to its original form after the arrow is released. When a tennis racket hits a tennis ball, the shape of the ball is distorted or deformed, but it regains its original shape when it bounces off the tennis racket.\n\nNot all materials return to their original shapes when a deforming force acting on it is removed. Materials that do not return to their original shapes after being distorted are said to be inelastic. Examples of inelastic materials are plasticine, clay and dough.\n\nA tennis ball is flattened by the contact force exerted on it by the strings of the tennis racket. Likewise, the strings of the racquet are force exerted by the ball. deformed by the contact The two forces are\n\ninteraction partners.\n\nMost materials are elastic up to a certain Figure 7.9 limit known as the elastic limit. Beyond the elastic limit a material will not return to its original dimensions break or permanently deforms when the deforming force is removed.\n\n7.9.1 HOOKE'S LAW\n\nWhen a spring is stretched or compressed, the extension or compression is directly proportional to the applied force (Figure 7.9). This relationship is known as Hooke's law which states that within elastic limits the extension (or\n\nF\n\nF\n\nF\n\nrestoring\n\nX\n\n0\n\nrestoring\n\nX = 0\n\nrestoring\n\nX\n\nPage\n\n189\n\nProperties of Matter\n\ncompression) x is directly proportional to the restoring force Fri.e.\n\nFres x-x\n\nor\n\nFres = -kx\n\n7.6\n\nwhere k is known as the force constant having units N m\". The negative sign show\n\nthat force is directed against displacement. This relationship is also true for a wi\n\nunder tension.",
        "7.10 Stress, Strain and Young's Modulus\n\n7.10 STRESS, STRAIN AND YOUNG'S MODULUS\n\nA wire under tension will extend. Its extension depends on a number of\n\nfactors such as the tension, its diameter and length and the material of the wire\n\nSuppose that a wire of length land cross-sectional area 'A' is stretched by: force Fand the extension produced is x as shown in figure 7.10.\n\nStress:\n\nThe stress applied to the wire is defined as the force applied per unit area of cross-section to produce deformation. Mathematically\n\nFigure 7.10\n\nForce\n\nStress =\n\nArea of crossection\n\nor\n\nstress\n\nF\n\nA\n\n7.7\n\nStrain:\n\nThe unit for stress is N m2 or pascal.\n\nAs a result of the wire being stretched, it is under strain. The strain is defined as the extension per unit length. Mathematically\n\nor\n\nExtension Original Length\n\nStrain =\n\nX\n\nstrain =\n\n7.8\n\nSince strain is the ratio of two lengths, it does not have a unit.\n\nLAB WORK\n\nTo find the density of a liquid using 5 ml syringe (instead of dimity bottle).\n\nYoung's Modulus:\n\nThe strain produced in a stretched wire is proportional to the stress within\n\nPage\n\n190\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nthe limit of proportionality. Within this limit, the ratio is a constant whose value depends on the material of the wire, and is known as the Young's modulus of the\n\nmaterial. Mathematically\n\nor\n\nStress & Strain\n\nStress Constant > Strain\n\nConstant of proportionality is called Young's modulus is denoted by Y\n\nStress = Young's Modulus x Strain\n\nrearranging the above equation, we get\n\nModulus for Selected Materials\n\nYoung Modulus,\n\nMaterial\n\nY, (Pa)\n\nAluminum\n\n7.0 × 1010\n\nBrass\n\n9.0 × 1010\n\nCopper\n\n11 x 1010\n\nStress\n\nYoung's Modulus =\n\nCrown glass\n\n6.0 × 1010\n\nStrain\n\nIron\n\n21 × 1010\n\nYoung's modulus is denoted by Yand its unit is Nm2, putting values of stress and strain, we\n\nLead\n\n1.6 × 1010\n\nget\n\nY =\n\nF/A\n\nNickel\n\n21 × 1010\n\nX\n\nSteel\n\n20 x 1010\n\nFxl\n\nrearranging\n\nY =\n\n7.9\n\nAxx\n\nYoung Modulus for different materials are given in the table 7.2.\n\nStress strain curves:\n\nStress and strain curves are measured by stress tester.\n\nThe applied stress is increased and the change in length is noted. The values are then printed on graph. A typical graph for metal is shown in the figure 7.11.\n\nHere, Point A, is the limit of proportionality, the limit up to which Hooke's law is obeyed called proportional limit.\n\nLAB WORK\n\nFigure 7.11 Stress strain curve\n\nStress\n\nol\n\nElastic region B\n\nC\n\nA\n\nPlastic\n\nregion\n\nStrain\n\nTo study the relation between load and extension (helical spring) by drawing\n\ngraph.\n\nPage\n\n191\n\nProperties of Matter\n\npoint C is the maximum stress wire can with stand without breaking and point D, is Point B, is the elastic limit, the limit up to which material shows elastic behavior,\n\nthe breaking point, where material breaks.\n\nExample 7.5\n\nYOUNG'S MODULUS\n\nAwire of length 5.0 m and area of 0.03 m2 is stretched 0.15 m within elastic limit, by hanging weight of 270 N. Calculate stress, strain and Young's modulus of\n\nmaterial of wire.\n\nS GIVEN:\n\nO length = 5.0m\n\nL area A=0.03 m2\n\nU extension x = 0.15m\n\nTweight force F = 270 N\n\nF\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nstress=?\n\nstrain = ?\n\nYoung's modulus Y = ?\n\na) By definition of stress\n\nstress =\n\n=-\n\nO\n\nA\n\nN\n\nputting values\n\nstress:\n\n270N 0.03m2\n\nstress=9x103 Nm-2\n\nAnswer\n\nb) By definition of strain\n\nstrain =\n\nX\n\nputting values\n\n0.15m\n\nstrain =\n\n5.0m\n\nstrain = 0.03\n\nAnswer\n\nc) Young's Modulus is\n\nYoung's Modulus\n\n·Stress\n\nStrain\n\nputting values\n\nY = 9.0×103Nm-2\n\n0:03\n\ntherefore\n\nY = 3.0×105Nm-2\n\nAnswer\n\nAssignment 7.5 WIRE STRETCHED\n\nAn elastic wire of length 2 m and cross sectional area 0.02 m2 is stretched 0.10m\n\nby a 300 N weight. Calculate the Young modulus of the material.\n\nK Density: Mass per unit volume\n\nE Pressure: Force applied per unit area\n\nBarometer: Device used to measure atmospheric pressure\n\nPascal's Principle: if the pressure at one point of a confined fluid is increased by P an amount, the pressure increases by the same amount at all other parts 0 throughout the fluid.\n\nArchimedes' principle: A body when completely or partially immersed in a fluid will experience an upthrust equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the Tbody. This upthrust will bring about an apparent loss of weight of the body.\n\nP\n\nElasticity: The property of a body which enables the body to regain its original dimension when the deforming force acting on the body is removed.\n\nHooke's law: Within elastic limits the extension (or compression) is directly proportional to the force applied.\n\nStress: The force applied per unit area of cross-section to produce deformation. Strain: the extension per unit length.\n\nYoung's Modulus: The ratio of stress to strain.\n\nGROUP-A\n\nR TYRE PRESSURE: Research air pressures in different tyre types (bicycles, motor Obikes, cars and trucks). Why are the pressures different for different automobile\n\ntyres? Prepare a presentation to be presented in physics class.\n\nECTS\n\nGROUP - B\n\nSUBMARINES: Prepare a chart on submarine technology, what is the science of their\n\nsinking and raising up.\n\nGROUP-C\n\nWEATHER IN PAKISTAN: What is the organization that monitors and predicts weather in Pakistan, write a research article for the school library.\n\nGROUP-D\n\nPRESSURIZED CABINS: Write an article for school magazine on why submarine and aircraft cabins are pressurized at certain level. What would happen if the cabins are\n\nnot maintained at constant pressure.\n\nGROUP-E\n\nDENSITIES: Find a spring balance and use Archimedes Principle to determine the densities of few objects. Compare the calculated density results with the table and\n\nPage\n\n193"
