            "q": "Define electric field and electric field intensity. Derive the formula for the electric field intensity due to a point charge and describe the properties of electric field lines.",
            "ans": "1. Electric Field: The space or region around an electric charge in which another charge experiences an electrostatic force.\n2. Electric Field Intensity (E): The force per unit positive test charge placed at that point: E = F / q0 (SI unit: N/C or V/m).\n3. Derivation for Point Charge: By Coulomb's law, the force on test charge q0 at distance r from charge q is F = k * q * q0 / r^2. Substituting into E = F / q0 yields: E = k * q / r^2.\n4. Properties of Field Lines:\n- They originate from positive charges and end on negative charges.\n- Tangent to a field line gives the direction of electric field at that point.\n- Line density indicates field strength (closer lines = stronger field).\n- Two field lines never cross each other because the field has only one direction at any given point."
