          {
            "q": "Explain the force on a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field. Describe the factors on which it depends and Fleming's Left-Hand Rule.",
            "ans": "1. Principle: When a current-carrying conductor is placed in an external magnetic field, the magnetic field of the current interacts with the external magnetic field, producing a net magnetic force.\n2. Formula: F = I * L * B * sin θ, where:\n- I = current in the conductor (A)\n- L = length of conductor inside field (m)\n- B = magnetic field strength (Tesla)\n- θ = angle between conductor and magnetic field\n3. Factors affecting force: F ∝ I, F ∝ L, F ∝ B, F ∝ sin θ. Maximum at θ = 90°, zero at θ = 0°.\n4. Fleming's Left-Hand Rule: Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of the left hand mutually perpendicular to each other. If the Forefinger points in the direction of the Magnetic field (B) and the Middle finger points in the direction of the Current (I), then the Thumb indicates the direction of the Force (thrust) on the conductor."
          },
          {
            "q": "Describe the construction and working of a simple DC motor. What is the role of the split-ring commutator?",
            "ans": "1. Principle: Operates on the principle that a current-carrying rectangular coil placed in a magnetic field experiences a torque that causes it to rotate.\n2. Construction:\n- Armature: A rectangular coil of insulated copper wire wound on a soft iron core.\n- Field Magnet: Strong permanent magnet or electromagnet with concave curved pole pieces providing a radial magnetic field.\n- Split-ring Commutator: Two half-copper rings (segments) connected to the coil ends.\n- Carbon Brushes: Flexible carbon blocks that press against the split rings to supply current from a DC battery.\n3. Working: Current enters one half of the coil and leaves through the other. By Fleming's left-hand rule, opposite sides experience opposite vertical forces, creating a couple that rotates the coil.\n4. Role of Commutator: Reverses the direction of current in the coil every half rotation (180°), ensuring that torque acts in the same rotational direction continuously."
          },
          {
            "q": "Describe the phenomenon of mutual induction. Explain the principle, construction, and working of a transformer, including the transformer equations.",
            "ans": "1. Mutual Induction: The phenomenon in which a changing current in one coil (primary) induces an electromotive force (EMF) in an adjacent coil (secondary): εs = -M (ΔIp / Δt).\n2. Transformer Principle: An electrical device that increases or decreases alternating voltage based on mutual induction between two magnetically linked coils.\n3. Construction: Two coils—Primary (Np turns) and Secondary (Ns turns)—wound on a laminated soft iron core to guide magnetic flux with minimal energy loss.\n4. Working & Equations:\n- Alternating voltage Vp across primary produces alternating flux Phi in the core.\n- Induced primary EMF: Vp = -Np (ΔPhi / Δt).\n- Induced secondary EMF: Vs = -Ns (ΔPhi / Δt).\n- Dividing gives the Transformer Equation: Vs / Vp = Ns / Np.\n- For an ideal (100% efficient) transformer: Pin = Pout => Vp * Ip = Vs * Is => Is / Ip = Vp / Vs = Np / Ns.\n- Step-Up: Ns > Np => Vs > Vp (voltage increases, current decreases).\n- Step-Down: Ns < Np => Vs < Vp (voltage decreases, current increases)."
          }
