          "text": "In a monoatomic gas such as helium, single atoms move randomly and collide with each other and container walls; each has translational kinetic energy. Molecules of two or more atoms can also stretch, contract and spin, giving vibrational and rotational kinetic energy. Liquid molecules move freely within the liquid but attract each other, storing energy as they approach. Solid atoms jiggle rather than move around; they have kinetic energy and potential energy in the strong attractive forces holding them together. Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of particles. Other contributions to internal energy do not affect temperature. Particle kinetic energy may be translational, vibrational or rotational. At higher temperatures molecules move more and have greater kinetic energy. Figure 8.1 compares low and higher average particle kinetic energy after energy is added to a gas.",
