          "text": "The change in length Δl is proportional to original length l₀. For the same temperature rise, a 4 m iron rod lengthens twice as much as a 2 m rod: Δl∝l₀ (8.2). Since Δl∝ΔT, combining gives Δl∝l₀ΔT, or Δl=αl₀ΔT (8.6). α is the coefficient of linear thermal expansion for the material, in °C⁻¹ or SI K⁻¹. With Δl=lₜ−l₀, lₜ=l₀+αl₀ΔT=l₀(1+αΔT) (8.7). If ΔT=T−T₀ is negative, Δl is negative and the rod shortens. The coefficient α is increase in length per unit original length per kelvin rise: α=Δl/(l₀ΔT) (8.8). In words, it is the increase of a 1 m wire for a 1 degree rise. α depends on the material; values are in Table 8.2. Figure 8.5 labels the original length l₀, heated length lₜ and change Δl.",
