          "text": "Equal-volume aluminum and lead blocks are heated to equal temperatures then put on paraffin wax. Aluminum melts more wax and releases more heat although lead is about four times heavier, because lead’s specific heat is about seven times smaller. EXAMPLE 8.5: Find heat to raise 0.5 kg water (c=4190 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) from 10 °C to 65 °C. Printed working gives T₀=283 K, T=338 K, ΔT=55 K, but substitutes 50 K: ΔQ=cmΔT=4190×0.5×50=104750 J. ASSIGNMENT 8.5: 117.60 J raises 10 g silver by 50 °C; calculate silver’s specific heat. LAB WORK: Find specific heat by mixture method using polystyrene cups of negligible heat capacity.",
