          "text": "Before heating, iron atoms vibrate about equilibrium positions. Near a flame they vibrate faster and over wider distances, collide with neighboring atoms and transfer energy onward. Metals also contain free electrons that move through them: heated atoms transfer kinetic energy to free electrons, which move faster and diffuse into cooler regions, colliding with other electrons and atoms to transfer energy. Conduction is transfer of heat energy from particle to particle. Good conductors allow heat through easily; poor conductors (insulators) do not. ACTIVITY 9.1 compares metals: rods of copper, aluminum, brass and iron are coated with wax and fixed to matchsticks; heat their other ends. Wax melts farther on copper; matchsticks fall as wax melts. Conductivity order shown: copper, aluminum, brass, iron.",
