          "text": "READABLE SOURCE TEXT ONLY—A page-turn obstruction covers part of the printed page, including some of the Water activity. The visible text says metals are conductors; wood, rubber, plastic and fiberglass are poor conductors. Activity 9.1 compares same-size metal rods soldered into a tank and coated with wax; boiling water melts wax to different lengths, with more melting on copper, showing copper conducts well. Conduction occurs in solids, liquids and gases; solids conduct better than liquids and gases are worst. Metallic solids conduct best because of closely connected atoms and free electrons; non-metallic solids lack free electrons and conduct poorly. Most liquids conduct poorly because molecular distances are larger and collisions less frequent than in solids. Water is a poor conductor. Gases are poorest because particles are far apart; air has about one-twentieth water’s thermal conductivity. Wood, cloth, fiberglass and plastic foam insulate because they contain small pockets of trapped air. The covered activity wording and illustration details remain unverified.",
