          "text": "Good and poor thermal conductors have useful applications. A. Cooking pots and pans are metal, good conductors that readily transfer heat to food and spread it evenly. B. Plastic foam and fiberglass insulators in home walls/ceilings keep homes cool in summer and warm in winter; trapped-air pockets insulate. C. Wire gauze over a flame conducts heat outward so a glass beaker can be heated safely without concentrated flame heat. D. Cloth or wooden pot holders/table mats are poor conductors and prevent burned hands. E. Woolen clothes have fine air-filled pores; air and wool conduct poorly, keeping body heat from escaping in winter. F. Igloos made from ice and snow protect from winter weather because ice/snow insulate and reduce heat loss by conduction. G. An ice box has double tin/iron walls with cork (a poor heat conductor) filling the space. Figures 9.4a–e show a pot, pot holders, wool clothing, igloo and ice box.",
